From f078ddf4e8279c3007fca7b16cd316fd1b9d2ea4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: genspark_dev <genspark_dev@genspark.ai>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 03:42:51 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Index attachments from the framework's File document, and
 stop hiding its panel

The previous commit hid Frappe's own attachments panel with a DOM selector so the table
would be the only visible list. That reached into markup the framework does not publish and
worked against it rather than with it.

Frappe has one way to attach a file to a document - a File row carrying attached_to_doctype
and attached_to_name - and every door goes through it: the sidebar panel, drag-and-drop, the
REST upload endpoint, a row's own Attach control. So the app now listens there instead. Two
File events, registered through the framework's own doc_events hook, refuse a file on a
sealed record before it is stored and append a row for a record-level upload.

attached_to_field is what separates the two kinds of upload: frappe/client.py::attach_file
sets it from the docfield argument, so it is empty for an upload aimed at the record and
holds the field name when the user uploaded into a specific field. A field upload is the
user filling that row themselves, so it is left alone.

The row is written through the DocType's own controller - append then save - so the
duplicate check, the seal guard, the file hash and the uploading user's write permission all
still apply. Nothing writes a child table directly.

Consequences:

* The panel is restored and the custom upload button is gone: one upload affordance, the
  framework's own, and it now feeds the table instead of competing with it.
* A sealed record refuses an attachment at the framework level, so the rule holds for the
  REST endpoint too, not only for a button the interface had hidden.
* Files uploaded before this existed are indexed by a patch. It plans first and prints what
  it wrote, is idempotent, and deliberately leaves sealed records alone - their stored hash
  covers their rows, so writing one would break the seal check on a record nobody touched.
